Private integer storage promotion — 2026-10-16

This bounded C-lowering pass promotes fixed, unescaped integer cells across
joins and loops. It admits complete initialized one-word integer record
copies, including a checked store to an external destination. It retains IR
allocation, lifetime, pointer and bounds checks. Partial representations,
padding, pointer records, volatile/atomic accesses, opaque assembly, escaped
objects and repeated allocation sites stay in memory. Every predecessor
contributes address and initialization facts. This is not certified EqSat.

The canary is removed only when every fixed object was promoted, with no
dynamic allocation or activation address. An explicit input-IR canary mark
is still honored. Unused native fixed addresses reserve no slot; dynamic
allocation remains observable. An alignment fixture now makes its addresses
live, and the instruction-table fixture reflects this changed DCE contract.
The first full gate retained those two fixture failures. The corrected
fixtures and implementation pass the combined full-workspace gate.

